        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128214; TODAY'S FOCUS</div>
        <div style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 10px;">A right is something you may do. A duty is something you owe. A free people needs both.</div>
        <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;">A government is only as good as the people who use it. In this lesson you will see the rights Texans hold, how many Texans were once kept from using them, and why a good <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A member of a country or state who has rights and also duties.">citizen</a> does more than wait for a turn to vote.</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#127919; BY THE END OF THIS LESSON YOU WILL BE ABLE TO&hellip;</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">1</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> name the rights Texans have, including voting, serving on a <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A group of citizens chosen to listen to a court case and decide the facts.">jury</a>, and free speech and press.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">2</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> explain how the <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A fee people had to pay before they were allowed to vote.">poll tax</a> and the white primary kept Texans from voting, and how they were ended.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">3</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> describe what <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A group of people with similar ideas who work together to win elections.">political party</a> and <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A group of people who work together to influence leaders on an issue they care about.">interest group</a> do, and explain why taking part is a responsibility.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128203; WHAT YOU NEED FOR THIS LESSON</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px; border-left: 3px solid #D0D9E8; padding-left: 14px;"><span style="color: #c7922c; font-size: 18px; flex-shrink: 0;">&#9654;</span>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.6;">This page.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 1 &middot; YOUR RIGHTS</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Rights Texans Hold</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The Texas Bill of Rights is the first article of the state constitution, which tells you how much the delegates of 1876 cared about it. It guarantees many rights. Three are worth a close look.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>The right to vote.</strong> Today, a Texan who is a citizen, is at least 18, and has registered ahead of time may vote. Voting is how the people choose who holds the offices of government. In Texas, voters choose more officials than in most places, from the governor to the local judge and the school board.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Jury service.</strong> The Constitution guarantees a trial by <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A group of citizens chosen to listen to a court case and decide the facts.">jury</a> in many cases. A jury is made up of ordinary citizens, picked from among the people of the county. They hear the evidence and decide the facts. Serving on a jury is called a right, but it is also a duty. If the people will not serve, the courts cannot work. A person who receives a jury summons is required to answer it.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Free speech and a free press.</strong> The Texas Bill of Rights protects the right to speak, write, and publish on any subject, while leaving people responsible for any misuse of that right. A free people needs to be able to argue about the way it is governed, and to read the facts in print.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 2 &middot; THE FIGHT FOR THE BALLOT</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Who Was Kept Out</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">These rights were not open to every Texan. After Reconstruction ended, Texas leaders found ways to narrow who could vote.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">In 1902, Texas adopted a <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A fee people had to pay before they were allowed to vote.">poll tax</a>. A person had to pay a fee in order to vote. The fee was small to some people and large to others. Many Black Texans, many Mexican Americans, and many poor white farmers could not easily afford it. Voter turnout dropped sharply.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">There was another barrier. In Texas, the Democratic Party was so strong that the real contest was often settled in its <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="An election held before the main election, where a party chooses its candidates.">primary election</a>, where the party picked its candidate. Texas Democrats ran their primary as a private club, and they kept Black voters out. People called this the white primary. In 1944, Dr. Lonnie Smith, a Black dentist in Houston, challenged it. In <em>Smith v. Allwright</em>, the U.S. Supreme Court ruled that a party could not keep voters out of a primary because of race.</p><div style="margin: 16px 0;"><div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 8px;"><div style="min-width: 92px; font-size: 14px; color: #c7922c;"><strong>1902</strong></div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #0E1C42; border-radius: 6px; padding: 8px 14px; font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.5;">Texas adopts a poll tax.</div></div><div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 8px;"><div style="min-width: 92px; font-size: 14px; color: #c7922c;"><strong>1918 to 1919</strong></div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #0E1C42; border-radius: 6px; padding: 8px 14px; font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.5;">Women win the vote in Texas primaries, then Texas ratifies the 19th Amendment.</div></div><div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 8px;"><div style="min-width: 92px; font-size: 14px; color: #c7922c;"><strong>1944</strong></div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #0E1C42; border-radius: 6px; padding: 8px 14px; font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.5;">The Supreme Court strikes down the white primary in a Texas case.</div></div><div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 8px;"><div style="min-width: 92px; font-size: 14px; color: #c7922c;"><strong>1966</strong></div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #0E1C42; border-radius: 6px; padding: 8px 14px; font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.5;">A federal court ends the Texas poll tax.</div></div><div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 8px;"><div style="min-width: 92px; font-size: 14px; color: #c7922c;"><strong>1971</strong></div><div style="flex: 1; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 4px solid #0E1C42; border-radius: 6px; padding: 8px 14px; font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.5;">The voting age drops to 18.</div></div><div style="font-size: 12px; color: #6b7a99;">Steps in the fight over who could vote in Texas.</div></div><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Women had to fight for their right to vote as well. Texas women won the right to vote in primaries in 1918. In 1919, Texas became the first state in the South to approve the 19th Amendment. Later steps came more slowly. In 1966, a federal court struck down the Texas poll tax. In 1971, the voting age was lowered to 18.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">None of these rights was handed down. Ordinary people had to organize and take cases to court, often over many years, to make the law match its promise. Those people showed courage and perseverance, and Texas is a better place for it.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-seq" data-ra-skip data-next="0" data-done="None of these steps was handed down. Ordinary people organized and went to court, often for years, to make the law match its promise." data-hint="Not yet. Which of these came earlier?">
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; THE FIGHT FOR THE BALLOT</div>
  <div class="widget-question">These five steps changed who could vote in Texas. Click them in the order they happened.</div>
  <div class="seq-msg"></div>
  <div class="seq-pool"><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="3" data-date="1966" data-text="A federal court strikes down the Texas poll tax" data-fb="Voters no longer had to pay to vote.">A federal court strikes down the Texas poll tax</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="0" data-date="1902" data-text="Texas adopts a poll tax" data-fb="Many Black Texans, many Mexican Americans and many poor white farmers could not easily pay it.">Texas adopts a poll tax</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="4" data-date="1971" data-text="The voting age drops to 18" data-fb="More young Texans could take part.">The voting age drops to 18</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="2" data-date="1944" data-text="Smith v. Allwright ends the white primary" data-fb="Dr. Lonnie Smith, a Houston dentist, brought the case.">Smith v. Allwright ends the white primary</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="1" data-date="1918" data-text="Texas women win the vote in primaries" data-fb="A year later Texas became the first Southern state to approve the 19th Amendment.">Texas women win the vote in primaries</button></div>
  <div class="seq-line"></div>
  <div class="widget-feedback"></div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 3 &middot; TAKING PART</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Parties and Interest Groups</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Most elections in Texas are contests between <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A group of people with similar ideas who work together to win elections.">political party</a> candidates. A party is a group of people with similar ideas about government, who work together to win office. Through most of the 1900s, the Democratic Party was far the stronger in Texas. Today the two major parties are the Democrats and the Republicans. In a primary election, the voters of each party pick the person who will carry its name in the main election.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">An <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A group of people who work together to influence leaders on an issue they care about.">interest group</a> is different. It is not trying to win elections. It is a group that wants to influence what leaders decide about one issue or set of issues. Texas has groups for farmers and ranchers, teachers, doctors, oil and gas companies, builders, and many others. They <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="To try to persuade lawmakers to pass or reject a law.">lobby</a>, meaning they meet with lawmakers to argue for or against bills. Lobbying is protected by the right to petition the government, and it can give lawmakers useful facts. But a lawmaker has to weigh what a group says against what is good for everyone, since a group usually speaks for its own members.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">A Duty, Not Just a Right</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Think about the difference between a right and a duty. A right protects you from others. A duty is what you owe to others. A <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A member of a country or state who has rights and also duties.">citizen</a> has both. A Texan owes obedience to the laws, taxes to pay for government, and service on a jury when called. A Texan also owes something less written down: to stay informed, to vote, and to take part in the life of the community.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The old thinkers who inspired the Constitution believed that a free government depends on the habits of its people. If citizens give up on government and leave it to a few, the few will run it. If citizens take part, government stays closer to the people. Many Texans in history gave years of their lives to win a right that you will be able to use. What you do with it is up to you.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-select" data-ra-skip>
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; RIGHT, DUTY, OR BOTH?</div>
  <div class="widget-question">A citizen has both rights and duties. For each row, decide which word fits best.</div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. Taxes are owed whether or not you like how they are spent." data-hint="Think about what a citizen owes to others.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">1. Paying taxes to support government.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">A right</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">A duty</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">Both a right and a duty</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="0" data-fb="Right. The Texas Bill of Rights protects free speech and a free press." data-hint="Think about what the Bill of Rights protects you from.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">2. Speaking and writing freely about how Texas is governed.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">A right</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">A duty</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">Both a right and a duty</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="2" data-fb="Right. Jury service is called a right, but the courts cannot work if people will not serve, so it is a duty too." data-hint="The lesson says this one fits both words. Which answer covers both?">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">3. Serving on a jury when you are called.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">A right</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">A duty</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">Both a right and a duty</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="0" data-fb="Right. Lobbying is protected by the right to petition the government." data-hint="Think about which word fits a freedom that protects you.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">4. Meeting with lawmakers to argue for or against a bill.</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">A right</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">A duty</button><button type="button" data-choice="2">Both a right and a duty</button></div>
  </div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>

    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 12px;">&#128170; WHAT TO CARRY FORWARD</div>
        <p style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0;">A right unused is a right in danger. As you read about Texans who held office, ask what they did with the rights they had, and who had to win those rights first.</p>
    </div>
</div>

